gia rudd admitted that one of the  worst parts of wearing the suit was when  he had an itch that needed scratching  the suit made it impossible so he  resorted to literally having people  on set punch the itchy spots in hopes of  deterring the discomfort  it looks like he could still enjoy  himself through the pain though when i  had that the audition had no idea how  big the character would be palm  clementiev had to spend a crazy three  hours in the makeup chair each day she  was shooting to transform into mantis  on top of that clementev said keeping a  straight face in the makeup chair was  super challenging as well  so how exactly did they accomplish this  exotic look small prosthetics at the top  of her forehead acted as marks for her  antennae and her bug-like eyes were  courtesy of huge black contacts  it looks cool but she was left feeling  super claustrophobic and uncomfortable  because of them  we do this great black war paint it's  like a tar  painted across my face ronin the accuser  looks incredible on screen  but how much work went into bringing him  to life well  lee pace spent countless hours sitting  in the makeup chair being painted a  periwinkle color  having veins painted in and of course  these  thick black bits on his face pace  described it like having tar on your  face  which doesn't make the whole acting  thing easy it was so painful and  restrictive his face couldn't move  naturally at all while it felt so stiff  and heavy  on top of all that he had purple  contacts and a headdress  torture much even just to do this is  like  two pounds six pounds ten pounds  when michael chiklis took on the role of  the thing in the first two fantastic  four films  cgi wasn't quite as advanced as it is  now  so he wore a full suit made out of latex  and rubber  it took two hours to get the full  costume on prosthetic teeth included  when it came to performing chicklis  struggled with such a stiff costume that  made simple tasks like sitting or  picking up